{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-120871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/0ac836789132a294051f6cbd0756f26407d002fa83a278b3b8825cfdd9af0b81.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{2}+3x-2$ and $y=x+1,$ shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-120871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/299608a54c0b5f568f32619722dd678af1388065f017620071ffb0473a686fa3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+3x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-({x}^{2}+3x-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-120871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-120871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-120871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+3)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(-{x}^{2}-2x-1)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}({x}^{2}-3x)\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-120871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-2x+3)\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a928b14cfe88f9aaf7b8cf5288a18f3e81e163ea65fd3a48653fcba630c11386.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=8{x}^{2}-5$ and $y=1-2x,$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/226ade4a3b546484ba2e9ce5f266391fb51e9fe4b62e2e78d12200ac54ecea9a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(1-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1/3}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}-x-4)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}+x+5)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121424" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c77f7a71ccb7b4ad1598b09bf4fe585c82a7f614ebb4abe3f495643a746fcb3c.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2},$ shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9e6e1147d95c65465c802e8bfad5c4ac285dc9716d098452c41bdd6caed5b9ca.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\,(2-{x}^{2})-(-x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-80186" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+x+2)\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/9a87ae2b230e66b685051ee7d13987f5f68690a07a67ade9e7af0eb870f6224f.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/e77170aac6d81b1cfd0e93804b0c59802f28089d24aeaff065da04cabc44c172.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}[\\,(x+1)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}+x-1)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-x-1)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x-1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3121" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1}(x+1-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1e75b221cacd3d2bad25065fe13bbf77f07fa711fe7f865358ff181039ced560.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=-4$ and $x=8.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/56ec4a3e3bd4b8b652832d4b32ba730717a49ab2438ef2c978ea99b291a7f462.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}[\\,(x+6)-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x-8)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49880" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-4}^{8}(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+x+8)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}+2x-9$ and $y=2x+7?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw the graphs of the two functions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/2514c3e7bf5352dbe26dddc479f66042b3736b93f82b97a60850f768a89a4e17.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-9 & =2x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/560fb0937828d5afb5de9593280a1177773598c5d3534ee26318e0bc2788f5ab.png)\n\n \n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+2x-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2x+7.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(2x+7)-({x}^{2}+2x-9)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-{x}^{2}+16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(4-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{1}({x}^{2}-4)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16+4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121526" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=4x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b25bee7ef7257a5611d708eb65b3c8b453b230ad6d2e9289f6cb40dffba89277.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{2}-5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=5.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/bf79cec9c1ede4a95b3e20415fbf37224ca8616426e8530043fa4a4b54780bab.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}[\\,(4x)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+4x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{4}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{5}({x}^{2}-4x-5)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11487" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(5+4x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b5e422ab61b4ebcacafcadc082a1b85ebb453a689654a9cef7bc7e7a3a4721a3.png)\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2$ and $y=g(x)=3x-2$, shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x+2 & =3x-2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7d96f51f01c2e52756fe53a6b3a11dd5c7c6dd9cb15ce674a706b442a3e6ad2b.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[\\,(3x-2)-(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-6x+4)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(2{x}^{2}-x-2)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{4/3}(3x-2)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{4/3}^{2}(2{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-2{x}^{2}+6x-4)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=4{x}^{2}-x$ and $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/913ec93445f3ed571fcf98ad762eba2a1bc657dce7442c8e48fc4d5f36817def.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-x & =2-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-2)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/77493917a13269c78acef8ff6fe57b5f350020542805e316f82f7a17dabedd24.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}[\\,(2-{x}^{2}-4x)-(4{x}^{2}-x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(2-{x}^{2}-4x)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(4{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-5}^{2}(2-{x}^{2}-4x)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-5}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-49885" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(-5{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=3{x}^{2}-5x$ and $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/813677aa1b12481e990058b129cb9ad24945a3dfb818afacfb2ecb720d6b81d3.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5x & =6-2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 11}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{6}{5}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/a8c538d5f40f68506588695aa04b38f095360b6f51369d8169099806a9678de9.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=3{x}^{2}-5x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6-2{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}[\\,(6-2{x}^{2}-4x)-(3{x}^{2}-5x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1/5}({x}^{2}+x+3)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2/5}(5{x}^{2}+x+4)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1/5}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}-x+2)\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+5x-6)\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121512" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{6/5}(-5{x}^{2}+x+6)\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-346621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-346621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-346621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-346621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-346621" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{256}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/c61d9140e5b8e57c8f75b532c6e26e22faa073e6631984a8f6a7b6f4280882e3.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region enclosed between $y={x}^{2}+4x$ and $y=x,$ shown above.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ x(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/6b2232d5a2853b22c731c317b365822e371f3d419bfcb3e528a07f211a372553.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+4x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[\\,(x)-({x}^{2}+4x)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-3)^{2}] \\\\  & =(0)-(9-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "\\frac{27}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-3146" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-9$ and $y=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/85961197a76cc38713537f80a9c97bb158247c71a8be4633c98245ea158634b4.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/7543876e9138be135630313317e162c9b256713f2565b0cd3b5a24bb9ba9a3b3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-9,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=7.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(7)-({x}^{2}-9)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{157}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{250}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{256}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{32}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-121580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{256}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ and $y=3{x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/4e3758ddf652c3dd7df10d381b42ca1dde50d48250bed672b740329191202597.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/54ad3b4c8bc3aef707af764cd8d8badf9390737dc75491aa66ea554f531a7576.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4{x}^{2}-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe can now set up the integration with our $x$ values (Top-Bottom):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[\\,(3{x}^{2})-(4{x}^{2}-1)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the integrand is an even function. Therefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2((1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3})-0) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-11488" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-351944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}+6$ and $y=10.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-351944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5ba7f7b4022c35c4afe7d285c57f87d6fcebfbcd8373ddc530f849e29fbc22de.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3ff79baa7d3d934dd9815b398cbd9f56990011370408ddede51e3c4110b4978c.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}+6,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=10.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(10)-({x}^{2}+6)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-351944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2c1e0d792f1f10c6cdeeed4eaa5bca11fc044459e0de27e8f0bc4fd7310c8b69"} {"question" "q-351944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "q-351944" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{32}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/state.json" "file_sha256" "a4f566fe5b868e16445b3b3c4e2805f15b968d71b31ac0987a24cefb9f8ce4c7"} {"question" "e-552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/b98474e6de2c94876a75758d56a7fb356de22bccd029bfeed0207affebab09ce.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-552.json" "file_sha256" "1e7de6cab7b8571c0cecff23d2d1ffa06d0c6983123e1aae0b6d6bcd5d5c8c92"} {"question" "e-552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1.$ So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/1b1c2b1826fa988ba23f7270b4c8bbdc91bf03066e8ca3638def329466d5b93e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt{x})-({x}^{2})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-552.json" "file_sha256" "1e7de6cab7b8571c0cecff23d2d1ffa06d0c6983123e1aae0b6d6bcd5d5c8c92"} {"question" "e-554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-554.json" "file_sha256" "5a39c40073806cd23fe2af25723dd5e6076f82ad4d4eb81d906b803de32bbc25"} {"question" "e-554" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/5f6980e3a7e4c7d1a5cc5d9b93a0110764b34ea6b7a4bec7c828f04cef842f7f.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/3c52f1405079380459e60ad35e05218d1f0468ee177f1e383498c672d48eaa8d.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(x+9)-({x}^{2}+x+5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-554.json" "file_sha256" "5a39c40073806cd23fe2af25723dd5e6076f82ad4d4eb81d906b803de32bbc25"} {"question" "e-553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/01f139c53b6328341b463f5ad941d37fccb241fff109c0584207017341e8e5f7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-553.json" "file_sha256" "7101d3391ae1733d3f7a40764abedf4707006c210a91d31af11d0e40044247a2"} {"question" "e-553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/assets/39798ef4ff6e898527864628dc2750f88b115c03b2d6b8fb729f30b21d2d2bf5.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{2}-2x+2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3})-(0-0) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013841/example-553.json" "file_sha256" "7101d3391ae1733d3f7a40764abedf4707006c210a91d31af11d0e40044247a2"}] "usage" {"e-553" [] "e-554" [] "q-49885" [] "e-552" []} "basis" 1173 "retractions" [[17592186069313 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nWhich integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y = 4x^{2} - x$ and $y = 2 - x^{2} - 4x$?"] [17592186068532 :question/problem "Write down an integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/0af8c0419c649a2986c7a4112399a532.png)"] [17592186068532 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the intersection points between the two graphs occur when $x=0$ and $x=1$. So these will be our limits of integration.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/e2dad27370be8b6a9b82b1adc02d06c5.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{1}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}[(\\sqrt{x}) - (x^{2})]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x} - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068534 :question/problem "What is the integral that gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=x^2+x+5$ and $y=x+9$?"] [17592186068534 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/36b16b895b0234f1f9e10a91977d6c66.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + x + 5 &= x + 9 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/c27aaed348fa7bebc01f25f85b3a720e.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2+x+5$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x+9$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}[(x + 9) - (x^{2} + x + 5)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}(4 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186068533 :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region enclosed between $f(x) =x^2-2x+2$ and $g(x) = 2$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/5c6f110a048606921590ea4e6403ede0.png)"] [17592186068533 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2$.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/395/Source/Images/88219aab4954daef055e1fa24e9e55b6.png)\n\nThe vertical line\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=x^2-2x+2$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2$.\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{0}^{2}[(upper function) - (lower function)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}[(2) - (x^{2} - 2x + 2)]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(-x^{2} + 2x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(2x - x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3}) \\mid _{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (2^{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2^{3}) - (0 - 0) \\\\\n&= (4 - \\frac{8}{3}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186067410 17592186067411 17592186067412 17592186067413 17592186067414] "immutable_fields" [[17592186067409 :answer-field/correct] [17592186067409 :answer-field/id] [17592186067409 :answer-field/key] [17592186067409 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
